infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title Which awaits actually yield to the loop
  desc await is a may-suspend point, not a must — the left column suspends and returns control to the loop; the right column chains synchronously and runs start to finish in one task step (the starvation trap)
  compares
    - label Yields to the loop
      children
        - label await on real I/O
          desc Socket not yet readable — the future is pending, so the task step returns control
        - label await a real asyncio.sleep
          desc A timer on the heap; the loop sleeps until it is due
        - label await an unfinished task or future
          desc Still pending, so a done-callback is attached and the step returns
        - label await asyncio.sleep(0)
          desc The explicit yield point you insert to break a tight loop
    - label Chains synchronously, no yield
      children
        - label await a plain coroutine
          desc Just a synchronous call one frame deeper
        - label await an already-completed future
          desc Returns immediately without suspending
        - label 100 percent cache-hit fast path
          desc async but never yielding — runs start to finish in one task step
        - label A tight loop of these starves I/O
          desc One uninterruptible callback holds the loop for its whole duration
theme
  palette #14b8a6 #f43f5e
